#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85daf819-c485-5b76-9b07-bac1a22b4c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292768} {:answer/id #uuid "9f57052c-5479-5e54-bf26-0da42f1ae4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292769} {:answer/id #uuid "b10e3352-8230-5606-9b92-938172d27c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292770} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe63a00-9d88-54e1-a08f-f0e2fcaa50c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292771} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7d414f-7314-5ebc-9fe3-07d4c12d3519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85daf819-c485-5b76-9b07-bac1a22b4c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292768} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac18cc15-e783-5eb8-8c37-9be083dc6b70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0165882d-d9e6-5904-8c7d-cc258a668fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125129" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-{x}^{3}}$ at $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(1).\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{3}}+3{x}^{4}{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{-{1}^{3}}+3\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+3{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67981fba-8b7b-5aca-ae3c-86c44d2d765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348045} {:answer/id #uuid "9978aa49-945d-5279-a0f2-c5bf0f2458c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348046} {:answer/id #uuid "0cefc7e7-93ae-52cc-8d05-c2c58f0a0b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348047} {:answer/id #uuid "cfacc6ac-8414-5223-96d0-45d26a13bcf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348048} {:answer/id #uuid "21d1ec86-5f56-57db-a9e5-b8d22bfc00aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21d1ec86-5f56-57db-a9e5-b8d22bfc00aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348049} :answer-field/id #uuid "48ba0d34-03aa-5c01-9d1a-e255f69d5396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f9cde73-40ae-547d-802a-7ec567609306" :question/math-academy-id "q-15639" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{3}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b314e82c-c376-5f16-a296-e0f1868064c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b314e82c-c376-5f16-a296-e0f1868064c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348052} :answer-field/id #uuid "078a3e91-6106-5938-859d-451323231709" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5e4091b-73f7-57b4-8f45-dec53f6acfb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5e4091b-73f7-57b4-8f45-dec53f6acfb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348054} :answer-field/id #uuid "38abe5a2-3c2e-5153-8bfc-061d892a6ba8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7798ecc6-3643-5cec-9857-5b025e3b6aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7798ecc6-3643-5cec-9857-5b025e3b6aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348056} :answer-field/id #uuid "d064edb9-0923-55ee-a1b4-e037e0f6eaf6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0cf8efa-6fc7-5bd2-bd5d-4aeaac7ec023" :question/math-academy-id "q-347466" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}\\,x}$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$\\,{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}\\,x}=(1-\\sqrt{2}\\,x)^{-1} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{2}(1-\\sqrt{2}\\,x)^{-2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4(1-\\sqrt{2}\\,x)^{-3} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+\\sqrt{2}\\,x+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\sqrt{2}\\,x+2\\,{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1a8b306-86e6-5d55-be87-1e3917b8a5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298847} {:answer/id #uuid "7e84e586-4594-557e-8a58-7bb94ad7af1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298848} {:answer/id #uuid "e36dd7bb-8f18-5419-96b1-0abf0db3034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298849} {:answer/id #uuid "35dc4de5-8c28-558c-a14f-6835fc292143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298850} {:answer/id #uuid "0b56f66e-39cc-5d76-b8ee-98cffe2ba845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1a8b306-86e6-5d55-be87-1e3917b8a5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298847} :answer-field/id #uuid "7242b31d-06a4-5a77-bbf0-3eae604e6c07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a000ea29-976d-55b5-925a-b906444d88a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81059" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c3e4a6f-bb35-5f43-b43d-e91147eac604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348059} {:answer/id #uuid "6234f610-391c-5987-8321-7b60d3b19e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348060} {:answer/id #uuid "e131caab-868e-50ca-b2b4-233299b291d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348061} {:answer/id #uuid "4fce059c-05a0-5ca8-a7a1-36aa6f74e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348062} {:answer/id #uuid "c4669e03-32fe-58aa-8503-d66e52690501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6234f610-391c-5987-8321-7b60d3b19e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348060} :answer-field/id #uuid "386f2634-6a02-5424-a30e-4354f55e1520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1446f41e-fdd3-5f5c-9f6f-940b5c967764" :question/math-academy-id "q-81060" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\pi )+{f}^{′}(\\pi )(x-\\pi )+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\pi )}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\pi )}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
